WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.461
Ultrasound questions become much calmer once the learner refuses to treat ultrasound as a mysterious new beam.

2
00:00:06.461 --> 00:00:12.922
Ultrasound is sound with frequency above the usual human hearing limit.

3
00:00:12.922 --> 00:00:19.383
That means it still travels in a medium, still obeys the wave relation, and still reflects from boundaries.

4
00:00:19.383 --> 00:00:25.844
The classification comes first, the wavelength calculation comes second, and the scanning application only makes sense after those two steps are secure.

5
00:00:25.844 --> 00:00:32.305
That order is what keeps this lesson from feeling scattered.

6
00:00:32.305 --> 00:00:38.766
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 4 becomes far easier once the learner keeps frequency-band classification and sound-wave identity together.

7
00:00:38.766 --> 00:00:45.227
Classification means above 20 kHz.

8
00:00:45.227 --> 00:00:51.688
Ultrasound means sound whose frequency sits above the usual human hearing range.

9
00:00:51.688 --> 00:00:58.149
Identity means still sound.

10
00:00:58.149 --> 00:01:04.610
Crossing the hearing boundary changes the band label, not the physical identity of the wave.

11
00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:10.719
This diagram matters because it places the hearing boundary and the application story on the same board.

12
00:01:10.719 --> 00:01:16.828
One region covers ordinary hearing.

13
00:01:16.828 --> 00:01:22.937
Beyond that lies ultrasound.

14
00:01:22.937 --> 00:01:29.046
The change is in frequency band, not in the fact that the wave is sound.

15
00:01:29.046 --> 00:01:35.154
That is why the same diagram can show both classification and reflection from boundaries.

16
00:01:35.154 --> 00:01:41.263
Once the learner reads it that way, the scanner example later stops feeling like an unrelated medical trick.

17
00:01:41.263 --> 00:01:47.372
Keep this takeaway in view: A strong ultrasound explanation classifies the band first and preserves the 'still sound' label all the way through.

18
00:01:47.372 --> 00:01:53.481
Audible band: Most human hearing sits roughly between twenty hertz and twenty kilohertz.

19
00:01:53.481 --> 00:01:59.590
Ultrasound band: Above the audible limit, the sound is classified as ultrasound.

20
00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:05.901
A rising-whistle analogy helps because it shows what really changes at the hearing boundary.

21
00:02:05.901 --> 00:02:12.212
If the note climbs high enough, a person may stop hearing it, but the wave is still a sound wave travelling in the medium.

22
00:02:12.212 --> 00:02:18.523
The classification changed because of the listener's hearing range, not because the wave became a different physical phenomenon.

23
00:02:18.523 --> 00:02:24.834
That is the right emotional tone for this lesson: crossing the band boundary should feel like a label shift, not like a mystery.

24
00:02:24.834 --> 00:02:31.146
Keep this takeaway in view: The analogy makes the hearing-boundary shift feel simpler and more physical.

25
00:02:31.146 --> 00:02:37.457
What changes means the hearing label.

26
00:02:37.457 --> 00:02:43.768
The sound moves from audible to ultrasound because its frequency rises.

27
00:02:43.768 --> 00:02:50.079
What stays means sound identity.

28
00:02:50.079 --> 00:02:56.390
The wave is still sound in a medium even when a person can no longer hear it.

29
00:02:56.390 --> 00:03:03.133
The first move is to classify the sound correctly.

30
00:03:03.133 --> 00:03:09.877
Fifteen kilohertz is still inside the usual hearing band, while four point zero megahertz is far above twenty kilohertz and is therefore ultrasound.

31
00:03:09.877 --> 00:03:16.620
After the classification comes the wavelength calculation.

32
00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:23.363
Use wavelength equals speed divided by frequency in the medium.

33
00:03:23.363 --> 00:03:30.107
That gives a very small wavelength for the medical ultrasound wave, which is one reason the wave can be useful for reading structure in tissue.

34
00:03:30.107 --> 00:03:36.850
Keep this takeaway in view: The formula scene works when the learner treats classification and wavelength as two linked but separate moves.

35
00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:43.593
Classification step: Convert kilohertz or megahertz carefully before comparing with the twenty-kilohertz boundary.

36
00:03:43.593 --> 00:03:50.337
Calculation step: Use the sound speed in tissue and divide by the ultrasound frequency to get the wavelength.

37
00:03:50.337 --> 00:03:57.080
audible range is about 20 Hz to 20 kHz.

38
00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:03.823
ultrasound means sound above the usual audible limit.

39
00:04:03.823 --> 00:04:10.567
Classify the band first.

40
00:04:10.567 --> 00:04:17.310
Then calculate the wavelength using the speed in the medium.

41
00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:22.634
A strong routine for this lesson has three steps.

42
00:04:22.634 --> 00:04:27.958
First classify the frequency band and decide whether the sound is audible or ultrasound.

43
00:04:27.958 --> 00:04:33.282
Second calculate the wavelength using the speed in the medium and the sound-wave relation.

44
00:04:33.282 --> 00:04:38.606
Third explain the application by returning to the fact that ultrasound is still sound and can therefore reflect from boundaries.

45
00:04:38.606 --> 00:04:43.930
This order keeps the lesson scientific instead of letting the medical application swallow the wave physics.

46
00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:49.254
Keep this takeaway in view: Band first, wavelength second, application third is the safest routine for Lesson 4.

47
00:04:49.254 --> 00:04:54.578
Step 1 means classify the band.

48
00:04:54.578 --> 00:04:59.902
Compare the given frequency with the usual hearing range.

49
00:04:59.902 --> 00:05:05.226
Step 2 means calculate wavelength.

50
00:05:05.226 --> 00:05:10.550
Use lambda = v / f in the relevant medium.

51
00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:16.459
The medical scanning story only works because ultrasound keeps the ordinary sound-wave behaviors that matter here.

52
00:05:16.459 --> 00:05:22.368
It can travel through tissue, it can reflect when it meets a boundary, and its wavelength can be calculated with the same relation used for other sound waves.

53
00:05:22.368 --> 00:05:28.277
The impressive application should not hide the simple core idea.

54
00:05:28.277 --> 00:05:34.186
Ultrasound is powerful in medicine because it is high-frequency sound, not because it stopped being sound.

55
00:05:34.186 --> 00:05:40.094
Keep this takeaway in view: The application becomes easier to trust once the learner sees it as an ordinary-sound story at high frequency.

56
00:05:40.094 --> 00:05:46.003
Travel means through tissue.

57
00:05:46.003 --> 00:05:51.912
The wave still propagates as sound in a medium.

58
00:05:51.912 --> 00:05:57.821
Reflect means from boundaries.

59
00:05:57.821 --> 00:06:03.730
Different tissue boundaries can send part of the sound back to the probe.

60
00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:08.570
Run four checks.

61
00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:13.410
Ultrasound means sound above the usual hearing limit.

62
00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:18.250
Crossing that limit does not stop the wave from being sound.

63
00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:23.090
Wavelength still comes from speed divided by frequency in the medium.

64
00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:27.930
And scanner use depends on boundary reflection after the band has been classified correctly.

65
00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:32.770
Those four checks capture the lesson structure before the numbers appear.

66
00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:37.610
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can say why ultrasound is both high-frequency and still ordinary sound.

67
00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:42.450
Band means above 20 kHz.

68
00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:47.290
That is the key classification clue for ultrasound.

69
00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:52.130
Identity means still sound.

70
00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:56.970
The wave remains sound in a medium even when it is no longer audible.

71
00:06:56.970 --> 00:07:04.606
Start by converting the given frequencies into a comparable form and deciding which one lies above the hearing boundary.

72
00:07:04.606 --> 00:07:12.242
Then use wavelength equals speed divided by frequency with the sound speed in tissue to calculate the ultrasound wavelength.

73
00:07:12.242 --> 00:07:19.878
Finish by saying what the calculation means: the wave is ultrasound because of its frequency, and it can be used in scanning because it is still sound and can reflect from boundaries.

74
00:07:19.878 --> 00:07:27.514
That is the cleanest route into the worked example.

75
00:07:27.514 --> 00:07:35.150
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 4 becomes much safer once the learner keeps the 'still sound' idea active during every ultrasound calculation.

76
00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:42.786
Do not skip the band check: It tells you which sound is ultrasound before the calculation begins.

77
00:07:42.786 --> 00:07:50.422
Do not skip the meaning: Say that the wave is still sound and therefore still able to reflect from boundaries.

78
00:07:50.422 --> 00:07:58.058
classify the frequency band first.

79
00:07:58.058 --> 00:08:05.694
use lambda = v / f in the medium.

80
00:08:05.694 --> 00:08:13.330
The best answers preserve the phrase 'still sound' all the way through the application step.
